Venetium 0017 - Windows ARM32: platform compile blockers and ELF-directive guards.

BATCH-ARM-1 section 3A, as widened by the recon (ARM-1-PLAN.md) and his decision 3 (ARM-1-DECISIONS.md). These are what stop the
ARM32 compile from starting; one purpose: code that has an x86/x64/arm64 branch gets its Windows ARM32 (Thumb-2) branch. 18 files:
- The crash trap: base/immediate_crash.h, build/rust/allocator/immediate_crash.h, PartitionAlloc's partition_alloc_base/immediate_crash.h,
  pdfium core/fxcrt/immediate_crash.h, v8/src/base/immediate-crash.h fall to x86 int3/ud2 on Windows ARM32 (clang-cl defines _MSC_VER
  and __ARMEL__, not __GNUC__), which rt2.1 rejects for thumbv7; Windows ARM32 gets the trap instruction (udf #0xfe, as CR109 had it).
  The batch listed two of these five copies.
- ELF-only assembler directives (.type/.hidden/.size) guarded for COFF: PartitionAlloc's stack/asm/arm/push_registers_asm.cc and
  webrtc rtc_base/system/asm_defines.h.
- libvpx: ads2gas.pl gets -noelf -thumb on Windows, and run_perl.py forwards options to it (it forwarded none). Implicit IT blocks come
  from Venetium 0016's arm asm tool (-mimplicit-it=always), not per target.
- boringssl: OPENSSL_NO_ASM for is_win && current_cpu == "arm" (its ARM32 perlasm is __ELF__-only and assembles to empty objects on COFF).
- //base, media and perfetto compile blockers no list had: base/functional/bind_internal.h (the __stdcall/__fastcall specializations are x86-only,
  on ARM32 they redefine the plain ones), base/debug/stack_trace_win.cc (ARM32 CONTEXT: Pc/R11/Sp, IMAGE_FILE_MACHINE_ARMNT),
  base/debug/invalid_access_win.cc (Thumb nop is 2 bytes; no "ret" on 32-bit Arm), base/profiler/win32_stack_frame_unwinder.h (ContextPC),
  media/base/win/media_foundation_package_locator_helper.cc (the "arm" package directory); and, found by this patch's own compile
  check, the x86-only time stamp counter (__rdtsc) in base/time/time_win.cc, PartitionAlloc's time_win.cc and perfetto's time.cc,
  which excluded ARM64 only: the generic Windows-on-Arm branch (GetThreadTimes) now covers the whole Arm family.
Every hunk is Arm-scoped (ARCH_CPU_ARMEL, _M_ARM, current_cpu == "arm", or the Arm family where arm64 already took that branch):
the x86, x64 and arm64 builds do not change.

